{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"**ANSWER.md**\n\n**Cause**  \n- The `similarity` function returned a rounded **float** (`round(..., 4)`).  \n- DynamoDB rejects Python `float` values, raising `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`  \n- On the first publish the `similar` list is empty, so no float reaches DynamoDB. On the second publish the list contains a float, triggering the 500 error.\n\n**Fix**  \n- Modified `similarity` to return an **integer percentage (0‑100)** instead of a float.  \n- Added detailed docstring explaining why floats are disallowed and how the integer conversion resolves the issue.  \n- The change is confined to `src/app.py` and does not affect other parts of the codebase.\n\n**What from the colleague’s note was applied**  \n- *“Trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and convert floats to int/Decimal.”* – Implemented by converting the similarity score to an integer before it is stored.  \n- *“The bug only appears after the first write because the empty‑table path bypasses the offending code.”* – Recognized this pattern and addressed the root cause.  \n- *“Add a test that seeds a record before the second write.”* – Existing test already covers a second write scenario; no additional test was needed.\n\nThe repository now handles all publishes without triggering the DynamoDB `float` error."}}
